# GC116 I feel a lump below: Urinary incontinence in females, genital prolapse <span class="tag" tag-name="Lecture"><span class="smallcaps">Lecture</span></span>

- **Pelvic floor:**
  - All structures that are involved in supporting the pelvic cavity, not just the levator ani muscle
  - Peritoneum, pelvic viscera, endopelvic fascia, levator ani, perineal membrane and superficial genital muscles all form the pelvic foor
  - The female pelvis can be devided into atnerior, central/ apical, and posterior compartments

  
  ![](~/Documents/notes/img/female_pelvis.png)
- **Function of the pelvic floor:**
  - Support - maintainance of the normal position of pelvic viscera
  - Sphincteric function
  - Sexual function
- **Support of the lelvic floor** - 3 levels:
  - **Level 1 support** - damage to which results in vaginal vault prolapse:
    - Cardinal and uterosacral ligaments attach cervix and uterus to pelvic side walls
    - Downward extension of these ligaments to the upper vaginal enables attachment of vagina to the pelvic side wall (paracopium)
  - **Level 2 support** - damage to which results in rectocele, urethrocele and cystocele:
    - Paracopium surrounding midportion of vagina extends to pelvic side wall
    - Resulting fascia forms pubocervical fascia and rectovaginal vasia
  - **Level 3 support:**
    - Distal vagina directly attached to surrounding structures
    - Anteriorly fuses with urethra, posteriorly with perineal body, and laterally with the levator ani muscle
    - Laterally, levator ani muscles holds the pelvic floor closed by coapting the urogenital hiatus

